turns.text:
If the connection drops mid-transaction, the `client.query()` call that fails throws an error into the catch block. The catch block then tries `client.query("ROLLBACK")` on a dead connection, which also fails. That second error isn't caught, so it propagates up and masks the original failure.

The server doesn't know whether the query executed or not. If it dropped between the query and COMMIT, the query may have run on the database side while the client thinks the transaction was rolled back — the database connection hangs until it times out.

Wrap the ROLLBACK in try/catch to handle dead connections gracefully:

```javascript
} catch (err) {
  try {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  } catch (rollbackErr) {
    // Connection is dead; rollback will fail. Database will roll back on disconnect anyway.
  }
  throw err;
}
```

This preserves the original error and lets the database clean up on its own when the connection closes.

