No — raise the limit and it just leaks faster. `idle` stays at 0 while `waiting` climbs steadily in pool.log, meaning connections are checked out and never returned, not that legitimate demand exceeds 20.

The bug is in `withClient` (db.js:11-16): `client.release()` only runs if `fn(client)` resolves. Any query that throws leaks that client permanently.

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

